README - Unordered Array Operations (C++)

1. Aim:
To implement operations on an unordered array using C++ with a menu-driven interface, covering insertion, deletion, linear search, and array display.
2. Code Features:
2.1. Fixed-size array allocation of 100 integers.
2.2. Interactive menu loop using a do-while loop and switch-case statement.
2.3. Position validation for insertion and deletion to prevent invalid memory access.
2.4. Underflow check to ensure deletion is not attempted on an empty array.
3. Algorithm Steps:
3.1. Input: Read initial element count n and populate array arr.
3.2. Menu Loop: Present choices (1 to 5) repeatedly until choice 5 is selected.
3.3. Insertion (Choice 1): Validate position (0 to n). Shift elements right from index n-1 down to position. Insert new element and increment n.
3.4. Deletion (Choice 2): Check if empty. Validate position (0 to n-1). Shift elements left from position+1 up to n-1. Decrement n.
3.5. Search (Choice 3): Prompt for search key. Perform linear search from index 0 to n-1. Output match index or failure message.
3.6. Display (Choice 4): Iterate from index 0 to n-1 and print array elements.
3.7. Exit (Choice 5): End program execution.
4. Time Complexity:
4.1. Insertion: Best Case O(1) at end, Worst Case O(n) at start, Average Case O(n).
4.2. Deletion: Best Case O(1) at end, Worst Case O(n) at start, Average Case O(n).
4.3. Search: Best Case O(1) at first element, Worst Case O(n) at last or missing, Average Case O(n).
4.4. Display: O(n) as it traverses all active elements.
5. Space Complexity:
5.1. Auxiliary Space: O(1) using constant extra variables for indices and values.
5.2. Total Allocated Space: O(1) fixed allocation for arr[100].
6. Sample Input and Output:
Enter number of elements: 3
Enter elements: 10 20 30

MENU

1. Insert Element
2. Delete Element
3. Search Element
4. Display Array
5. Exit
Enter choice: 3
Enter element to search: 20
Element found at index 1.
